--&gt;&lt;noinclude&gt;{{dokumentacija}}&lt;/noinclude&gt;{{#invoke:quote|quote_t|type=book|hsb|title=Zhromadźene spisy. Zwjazk 7. Dramatika|year=1973|location=Budyšin|first=Jakub|last=Bart-Ćišinski|authorlink=Jakub Bart-Ćišinski|publisher=Domowina|origyear=1875|text={{{1|}}}|page={{{2|}}}}}&lt;noinclude&gt;{{dokumentacija}}&lt;/noinclude&gt;{{#invoke:quote|quote_t|type=book|hsb|title=Serbscy rjekowje. Powědańčko ze serbskeje zańdźenosće|year=1947|location=Bydišin|first=Jakub|last=Lorenc-Zalěski|authorlink=Jakub Lorenc-Zalěski|origyear=1922|edition=2|publisher=Koło Přećelow Serbskeho Pismowsta|text={{{1|}}}|page={{{2|}}}|pageurl=https://polona.pl/item-view/d2d263ca-5f76-4bdd-9557-91b283513585?page={{#expr:{{{2|}}}+3}}}}&lt;noinclude&gt;{{dokumentacija}}&lt;/noinclude&gt;{{#invoke:quote|quote_t|type=book|hsb|title=Bethlehemske dźěćatko a druhe legendy|year=1914|location=Budyšin|first=Mikławš|last=Bjedrich-Radlubin|authorlink=Mikławš Bjedrich-Radlubin|text={{{1|}}}|page={{{2|}}}}}&lt;noinclude&gt;{{dokumentacija}}&lt;/noinclude&gt;{{#invoke:object usage|show_obj}}&lt;noinclude&gt;{{dokumentacija}}&lt;/noinclude&gt;{{spódk dokumentacije}}
{{wužiwa lua|Modul:object usage}}
This template creates a description of the arguments that a term takes and their meaning.

==Parameters==
; {{para|1|req=1}}
: The language code of the term being defined. (See [[Wiktionary:Languages]].)
; {{para|2}}, {{para|3}}, ...
: Specs for the arguments that the term takes. See below.

===Arguments===
Parameters to {{tl|+obj}} indicate the arguments (direct and indirect objects, prepositions and associated cases, etc.) that a term (most commonly a verb, sometimes a noun, adjective or adverb) can take. A basic argument is either an ''inflection tag'' of the sort used in {{tl|infl of}} (see the documentation of that template), such as {{cd|dat}} (dative); or a ''literal word'' (e.g. preposition or conjunction) preceded by a colon, such as the German preposition {{cd|:auf}} or the Italian conjunction {{cd|:che}}. (Postpositions, as well as conjunctions whose associated clause precedes rather than follows, as in a head-final language like Japanese, should be preceded by two colons.) A literal word can be followed by an inflection tag in parentheses to indicate the ''governance'' of the associated object (i.e. what case or form it takes). Examples are {{cd|:auf(acc)}} ({{m+|de|auf}} + ''accusative'') and {{cd|:di(inf)}} ({{m+|it|di}} + ''infinitive''). For example, the German verb {{m|de|achten}} might be defined as follows:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[care]] about, to [[pay attention]] to {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which produces
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[care]] about, to [[pay attention]] to {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)}}

Either form of argument (inflection tag or literal word) can be followed by a gloss in angle brackets, e.g. {{m+|de|richten}} mgiht be defined as follows:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[direct]], to [[point]] (e.g. a weapon) {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)&lt;at&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which produces
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[direct]], to [[point]] (e.g. a weapon) {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)&lt;at&gt;}}

If a given argument can take more than one possible form with no difference in semantics, separate the possible forms with a slash (grouping them into an ''alternant set''). An example is {{m+|de|vertrauen}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[trust]], to [[place]] [[confidence]] {{+obj|de|dat/:in(acc)&lt;in someone/something&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which produces
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[trust]], to [[place]] [[confidence]] {{+obj|de|dat/:in(acc)&lt;in someone/something&gt;}}

If a given term can take more than one argument with different semantics, separate the arguments with a plus sign, as with {{m+|pl|truć dupę}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|pl|idiom|vulgar}} to [[bother]]; to [[pester]] {{+obj|pl|dat&lt;someone&gt; + :o(acc/loc)&lt;about something&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which produces
# {{lb|pl|idiom|vulgar}} to [[bother]]; to [[pester]] {{+obj|pl|dat&lt;someone&gt; + :o(acc/loc)&lt;about something&gt;}}
This also shows that a slash can be used inside of parens when a given literal word can govern an object in more than one possible case.

===Multiword arguments===
Examples shown so far use a single inflectional tag and a single literal word. In point of fact, however, the space character is not considered a separator, so you can freely use multiword inflectional tags and multiword &quot;literal words&quot;. A fairly simple example of the latter occurs with {{m+|it|armeggiare}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# to [[maneuver]]/[[manoeuvre]] or [[scheme]] {{+obj|it|:intorno a/:con/:su}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# to [[maneuver]]/[[manoeuvre]] or [[scheme]] {{+obj|it|:intorno a/:con/:su}}
Here, {{m|it|intorno a}} is a single compound preposition and is linked as such. If it is desired to link each word separately, surround them in double brackets, as with {{m+|bg|стига}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# [[provided]] (that), [[as long as|as long (as)]] {{+obj|bg|:да(clause)/:[[са́мо]] [[да]](clause)}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# [[provided]] (that), [[as long as|as long (as)]] {{+obj|bg|:да(clause)/:[[са́мо]] [[да]](clause)}}
Note also in this case that when non-Latin script literal words are used along with a governance tag, both the transliteration of the literal word(s) and the governance tag appear in the same set of parentheses.

When it is necessary to specify a multiword inflection tag, it is often best to join the individual words with {{cd|&amp;}} unless the combination with a space in it is recognized as an inflection tag in its own right. An example occurs with {{m+|zlw-opl|wrócić}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|zlw-opl|attested in|Masovia|Greater Poland|Lesser Poland}} to [[return]], to [[give back]] {{gl|to give something back to its original holder or owner}} {{+obj|zlw-opl|:w(acc&amp;poss&amp;pronoun)&lt;to whom&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|zlw-opl|attested in|Masovia|Greater Poland|Lesser Poland}} to [[return]], to [[give back]] {{gl|to give something back to its original holder or owner}} {{+obj|zlw-opl|:w(acc&amp;poss&amp;pronoun)&lt;to whom&gt;}}
The {{cd|&amp;}} says to treat each joined word or word set as an inflection tag and join them as if there were given as separate parameters to {{tl|infl of}} (which usually means with spaces). That is, the output of {{cd|acc&amp;poss&amp;pronoun}} is similar to the output of {{tl|infl of|zlw-opl|wrócić||acc|poss|pronoun}} (minus the lemma and preceding ''of'' at the end).

A special use of {{cd|&amp;}} is when placed before the beginning of the first inflection tag. This suppresses the preceding ''with''. This is useful with certain tags such as {{cd|transitive}}, such as with {{m+|ro|sări}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|ro|transitive}} to [[skip]] {{+obj|ro|&amp;transitive/:peste}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which displays as
# {{lb|ro|transitive}} to [[skip]] {{+obj|ro|&amp;transitive/:peste}}
This means that the object can either directly follow the verb or follow after the preposition {{m|ro|peste}}. Without the initial {{cd|&amp;}}, you'd get the following:
# {{lb|ro|transitive}} to [[skip]] {{+obj|ro|transitive/:peste}}
which doesn't look quite right.

===Multiple parameters===
If two or more arguments or argument sets with different semantics can co-occur, they should be separated with a {{cd|+}} sign, as shown above. However, if two such arguments ''cannot'' co-occur, there are two ways to handle this. One is to use separate parameters, as follows, for {{m+|it|accanirsi}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|it|intransitive}} to [[rage]] {{+obj|it|:su/:con/:contro&lt;against someone/something&gt;|:a(inf)&lt;against (doing something)&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|it|intransitive}} to [[rage]] {{+obj|it|:su/:con/:contro&lt;against someone/something&gt;|:a(inf)&lt;against (doing something)&gt;}}

Another way, which works well especially if there is only a single argument each, is to group them into an alternant set by separating them with a slash, giving each alternant its own gloss, as for another meaning of {{m|it|accanirsi}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|it|intransitive}} to [[persist]] {{+obj|it|:in&lt;in something&gt;/:a(inf)&lt;in (doing something)&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|it|intransitive}} to [[persist]] {{+obj|it|:in&lt;in something&gt;/:a(inf)&lt;in (doing something)&gt;}}

This second way could be done with the earlier example but would likely be confusing due to the multiple alternants in the first argument set, and cannot be done at all when a parameter consists of arguments or argument sets joined by {{cd|+}}. An example of this occurs with the transitive use of {{m+|grc|δέω}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
## {{lb|grc|transitive}} to [[beg]] {{+obj|grc|gen&lt;something&gt; + gen&lt;from someone&gt;|gen&lt;someone&gt; + inf&lt;to do something&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
## {{lb|grc|transitive}} to [[beg]] {{+obj|grc|gen&lt;something&gt; + gen&lt;from someone&gt;|gen&lt;someone&gt; + inf&lt;to do something&gt;}}

Another complex example where multiple parameters are necessary is with {{m+|grc|αἰσθάνομαι}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|grc|transitive}} to [[perceive]], [[apprehend]], [[notice]] {{+obj|grc|gen/acc&lt;something&gt;|nom&amp;part&lt;oneself doing, that one is doing&gt;|gen&lt;someone&gt; + gen&lt;doing something&gt;|:ὅτι(verb)/:ὡς(verb)&lt;that ...&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|grc|transitive}} to [[perceive]], [[apprehend]], [[notice]] {{+obj|grc|gen/acc&lt;something&gt;|nom&amp;part&lt;oneself doing, that one is doing&gt;|gen&lt;someone&gt; + gen&lt;doing something&gt;|:ὅτι(verb)/:ὡς(verb)&lt;that ...&gt;}}

===Qualifiers===
Qualifiers (and certain other inline modifiers) can be attached to any individual argument. Qualifiers take the form {{cd|&lt;q:...&gt;}} for a left qualifier (appearing directly before the argument) or {{cd|&lt;qq:...&gt;}} for a right qualifier (appearing directly after the argument). An example is {{m+|pl|brać}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|pl|reflexive-się}} to [[get]] to {{gl|to begin to do some activity}} {{+obj|pl|:do(gen)/:za(acc)&lt;q:proscribed&gt;&lt;to what&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which produces
# {{lb|pl|reflexive-się}} to [[get]] to {{gl|to begin to do some activity}} {{+obj|pl|:do(gen)/:za(acc)&lt;q:proscribed&gt;&lt;to what&gt;}}
Here, the qualifier {{cd|&lt;q:proscribed&gt;}} attaches to the argument {{cd|:za(acc)}} and appears directly before it.

The qualifiers {{cd|&lt;q:...&gt;}} and {{cd|&lt;qq:...&gt;}} are ''individual qualifiers'', meaning they are logically attached to a given individual argument and appear directly before or after it. There are also corresponding ''overall qualifiers'' {{cd|&lt;q*:...&gt;}} and {{cd|&lt;qq*:...&gt;}}, which logically attach to a ''alternant set'' of arguments (i.e. a set of arguments separated by a slash). These must appear after the last argument in the alternant set, and display slightly differently. For example, {{cd|&lt;q:...&gt;}} attached to the first argument in an alternant set appears ''after'' (inside of) the word ''with'' preceding the argument, while {{cd|&lt;q*:...&gt;}} attached the the end of an alternant set appears ''before'' (outside of) the word ''with'' preceding the arguments. The uses of these two types of qualifiers are different; for example, the above example could be further clarified as follows:
&lt;pre&gt; 
# {{lb|pl|reflexive-się}} to [[get]] to {{gl|to begin to do some activity}} {{+obj|pl|:do(gen)&lt;q:standard&gt;/:za(acc)&lt;q:common but proscribed&gt;&lt;to what&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
appearing as
# {{lb|pl|reflexive-się}} to [[get]] to {{gl|to begin to do some activity}} {{+obj|pl|:do(gen)&lt;q:standard&gt;/:za(acc)&lt;q:common but proscribed&gt;&lt;to what&gt;}}
Conversely, the following example for {{m+|pl|odpowiedni}} shows where {{cd|&lt;q*:...&gt;}} is appropriate}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{senseid|pl|suitable}} [[suitable]], [[appropriate]], [[proper]], [[adequate]], [[relevant]] {{+obj|pl|:do(gen)&lt;to what&gt;|dat/:dla(gen)&lt;for what&gt;&lt;q*:obsolete&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{senseid|pl|suitable}} [[suitable]], [[appropriate]], [[proper]], [[adequate]], [[relevant]] {{+obj|pl|:do(gen)&lt;to what&gt;|dat/:dla(gen)&lt;for what&gt;&lt;q*:obsolete&gt;}}

Finally, it should be noted that if an argument is not part of an alternant set (i.e. does not have a slash next to it), any individual qualifiers ({{cd|&lt;q:...&gt;}} and {{cd|&lt;qq:...&gt;}}) are automatically promoted to overall qualifiers. An example where this occurs is {{m+|ro|întâlni}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|ro|transitive|reflexive|or|reciprocal}} to [[meet]] {{+obj|ro|:cu&lt;q:if reflexive&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|ro|transitive|reflexive|or|reciprocal}} to [[meet]] {{+obj|ro|:cu&lt;q:if reflexive&gt;}}
The {{cd|&lt;q:...&gt;}} qualifier was automatically &quot;promoted&quot; to (i.e. converted into) {{cd|&lt;q*:...&gt;}}, causing it to appear outside (before) the preceding ''with''.

Another example where {{cd|q*}} is needed is the following definition of the Portuguese verb {{m|pt|prescindir}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|pt|intransitive|transitive|or|reflexive}} to [[do without]]; to [[dispense]] with {{+obj|pt|:de&lt;something&gt;/:de(inf)&lt;doing something&gt;&lt;q*:without or with a reflexive pronoun&gt;|dirobj&lt;something&gt;&lt;q*:without a reflexive pronoun&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|pt|intransitive|transitive|or|reflexive}} to [[do without]]; to [[dispense]] with {{+obj|pt|:de&lt;something&gt;/:de(inf)&lt;doing something&gt;&lt;q*:without or with a reflexive pronoun&gt;|dirobj&lt;something&gt;&lt;q*:without a reflexive pronoun&gt;}}
This verb comes in several syntactic constructions: (a) non-reflexive, with a direct object; (b) reflexive or non-reflexive, with the object preceded by {{m|pt|de}}; (c) reflexive or non-reflexive, with {{m|pt|de}} followed by an infinitive verb. The intransitive (non-reflexive) construction with {{m|pt|de}} appears the most common. This is expressed using a combination of qualifiers and alternants.

Arbitrary text can appear inside of qualifiers (as well as glosses and other inline modifiers surrounded by angle brackets). An example with {{m+|pt|bater à porta}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{senseid|pt|to present itself}} {{lb|pt|intransitive|idiomatic}} to [[come knocking]] {{gl|of a bad situation: to present itself to someone}} {{+obj|pt|:de(object)/a possessive pronoun&lt;q:preceding {{m|pt|porta}}&gt;&lt;for someone&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{senseid|pt|to present itself}} {{lb|pt|intransitive|idiomatic}} to [[come knocking]] {{gl|of a bad situation: to present itself to someone}} {{+obj|pt|:de(object)/a possessive pronoun&lt;q:preceding {{m|pt|porta}}&gt;&lt;for someone&gt;}}
Here, a template call to {{tl|m}} appears inside of angle brackets. This produces HTML that itself contains angle brackets, but this is parsed correctly because the parser ignores balanced angle brackets inside the inline modifier.

===Labels===
Labels, as in {{tl|lb}}, can be attached to any argument, just like qualifiers. The inline modifiers {{cd|&lt;l:...&gt;}} (for left labels) and {{cd|&lt;ll:...&gt;}} (for right labels) work parallel to left and right qualifiers. An example of such a label modifier in action is with {{m+|pt|estar}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{senseid|pt|forms the progressive}} {{lb|pt|auxiliary}} to [[be]]; {{ng|forms the [[progressive]] aspect}} {{+obj|pt|ger&lt;ll:Brazil&gt;/:a(inf)&lt;ll:Portugal&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{senseid|pt|forms the progressive}} {{lb|pt|auxiliary}} to [[be]]; {{ng|forms the [[progressive]] aspect}} {{+obj|pt|ger&lt;ll:Brazil&gt;/:a(inf)&lt;ll:Portugal&gt;}}
Here, the verb {{m|pt|estar}} has different syntax in Brazil and Portugal, and the labels auto-link to the appropriate Wikipedia article on the two dialects.

A similar example is with {{m+|pl|abdykować}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|pl|intransitive|law}} to [[abdicate]] {{gl|to surrender, renounce or relinquish, as a sovereign power}} {{+obj|pl|:na rzecz(gen)&lt;on behalf of someone&gt; + :z(gen)/:od(gen)&lt;l:in &lt;&lt;Middle Polish&gt;&gt;&gt;&lt;from what&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|pl|intransitive|law}} to [[abdicate]] {{gl|to surrender, renounce or relinquish, as a sovereign power}} {{+obj|pl|:na rzecz(gen)&lt;on behalf of someone&gt; + :z(gen)/:od(gen)&lt;l:in &lt;&lt;Middle Polish&gt;&gt;&gt;&lt;from what&gt;}}
This uses the double angle-bracket syntax described in [[Template:label#Double angle bracket notation]].

Another complex example follows, for {{m+|pt|dar}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|pt|&lt;&lt;transitive&gt;&gt; (&lt;&lt;Portugal&gt;&gt;) or &lt;&lt;intransitive&gt;&gt; (&lt;&lt;Brazil&gt;&gt;)|colloquial}} to [[defeat]] by a [[given]] [[score]] {{+obj|pt|dirobj&lt;ll:Portugal&gt;/:de&lt;ll:Brazil&gt;&lt;a score&gt; + :a&lt;ll:Portugal&gt;/:em&lt;ll:Brazil&gt;&lt;someone or someone's team&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|pt|&lt;&lt;transitive&gt;&gt; (&lt;&lt;Portugal&gt;&gt;) or &lt;&lt;intransitive&gt;&gt; (&lt;&lt;Brazil&gt;&gt;)|colloquial}} to [[defeat]] by a [[given]] [[score]] {{+obj|pt|dirobj&lt;ll:Portugal&gt;/:de&lt;ll:Brazil&gt;&lt;a score&gt; + :a&lt;ll:Portugal&gt;/:em&lt;ll:Brazil&gt;&lt;someone or someone's team&gt;}}

The distinction between ''individual'' and ''overall'' labels exists, exactly as for qualifiers, along with corresponding inline modifiers {{cd|&lt;l*:...&gt;}} and {{cd|&lt;ll*:...&gt;}} and corresponding &quot;promotion&quot; behavior for arguments not part of an alternant set.

===Arguments inside of parentheses===
The governance spec given in parentheses after a literal word (preposition, conjunction, or the like), which indicates the syntactic properties of the phrase or clause governed by the literal word, can be anything that goes in a single argument; it is processed recursively when parsing and formatting. Some examples were provided above where alternants can be given to indicate that a preposition can take a noun in more than one case, e.g. {{cd|:o(acc/loc)&lt;about something&gt;}}, and where multiple inflection tags separated by {{cd|&amp;}} are given, e.g. {{cd||:w(acc&amp;poss&amp;pronoun)&lt;to whom&gt;}}. A more complex example of this occurs with {{m+|pt|dar}} in its impersonal meaning &quot;to be possible&quot;:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|pt|impersonal}} to be [[possible]], [[can]] {{+obj|pt|:para(subject&amp;pronoun&lt;qq:optional&gt; + pers&amp;inf)&lt;for someone to do something&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|pt|impersonal}} to be [[possible]], [[can]] {{+obj|pt|:para(subject&amp;pronoun&lt;qq:optional&gt; + pers&amp;inf)&lt;for someone to do something&gt;}}
Another example with a somewhat complex argument inside parentheses occurs with {{m+|pt|saber}} in its meaning &quot;to know that ...&quot;:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{senseid|pt|to know a fact}} {{lb|pt|transitive|or|intransitive}} to be [[aware]], to [[know]] {{+obj|pt|:que(ind-cl&amp;when positive/sub-cl&amp;when negated)&lt;that ...&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{senseid|pt|to know a fact}} {{lb|pt|transitive|or|intransitive}} to be [[aware]], to [[know]] {{+obj|pt|:que(ind-cl&amp;when positive/sub-cl&amp;when negated)&lt;that ...&gt;}}
Another example with {{m+|az|oturub-durmaq}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# to [[dance to someone's tune]], to live and die by someone's word, to obey or follow every instruction from someone, to follow someone's guidance rather than taking independent decisions {{+obj|az|::ilə(:söz&lt;word&gt;/:dediyi&lt;what was said&gt;&lt;q*:mostly&gt;)&lt;with&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# to [[dance to someone's tune]], to live and die by someone's word, to obey or follow every instruction from someone, to follow someone's guidance rather than taking independent decisions {{+obj|az|::ilə(:söz&lt;word&gt;/:dediyi&lt;what was said&gt;&lt;q*:mostly&gt;)&lt;with&gt;}}
Here, in this usage of the term, the postposition {{m|az|ilə||with}} is usually accompanied by {{m|az|söz}} or {{m|az|dediyi}}.

Another example with {{m+|de|berufen}}:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[appoint]] {{+obj|de|acc&lt;someone&gt; + :auf(acc)/:zu(dat)&lt;to a high office&gt;/:als(acc&amp;if active/nom&amp;if passive)&lt;as someone&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[appoint]] {{+obj|de|acc&lt;someone&gt; + :auf(acc)/:zu(dat)&lt;to a high office&gt;/:als(acc&amp;if active/nom&amp;if passive)&lt;as someone&gt;}}

Another example with {{m+|de|bringen}}, which shows that nesting (in fact, arbitrary nesting) is possible inside of parens:
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[get]], to [[cause]] {{+obj|de|acc&lt;someone&gt; + :zum(nominalized&amp;verb)/:dazu(:zu(inf)/:dass(clause)/omitted&amp;clause)&lt;to do something&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
which appears as
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[get]], to [[cause]] {{+obj|de|acc&lt;someone&gt; + :zum(nominalized&amp;verb)/:dazu(:zu(inf)/:dass(clause)/omitted&amp;clause)&lt;to do something&gt;}}
The intent is that when {{m+|de|bringen}} means &quot;to cause&quot;, the verb expressing the action that is caused can be expressed either using {{m|de|zum}} followed by a nominalized verb or by {{m|de|dazu}}, which can optionally be followed by a {{m|de|zu}}-infinitive clause, a clause headed by {{m|de|dass||that}}, or nothing.

===Notes on inflection tags===
Inflection tags were originally created for use in {{tl|inflection of}}, which sometimes leads to mismatches between what is most convenient for the inflection tags used in {{tl|inflection of}} and those used in {{tl|+obj}}. Some things of note:
# The {{cd|direct}} tag refers to the {{w|direct case}}. For a direct object, spell out {{cd|direct object}} as a single tag or use its abbreviation {{cd|dirobj}}.
# Likewise the {{cd|indirect}} tag refers to the indirect case (see {{w|direct case}}). For an indirect object, spell out {{cd|indirect object}} as a single tag or use its abbreviation {{cd|indirobj}}.
# The abbreviations {{cd|adj}} and {{cd|adv}} stand for {{cd|adjectival}} and {{cd|adverbial}}, used as modifiers e.g. for participles in various Slavic languages. Spell out {{cd|adjective}} and {{cd|adverb}} to refer to these parts of speech.
# The tag {{cd|in}} is an abbreviation for {{cd|inanimate}}; to get the word ''in'', either use {{cd|!in}} or put it as part of a larger tag with embedded spaces.
# Shortcuts are provided for various types of clauses for use inside of parentheses to describe the part or speech governed by a given preposition or conjunction, e.g. {{cd|ind-cl}} for an indicative clause, {{cd|sub-cl}} for a subjunctive clause, {{cd|pres-cl}} for a present-tense clause, {{cd|pres-sub-cl}} for a present subjunctive clause, {{cd|cond-cl}} for a conditional clause, etc. See the documentation for {{tl|inflection of}} under [[Template:inflection of#Non-alias shortcuts]] for the full list.
# As a general rule, the part of speech aliases used in conjunction with the {{para|pos}} parameter to various templates (see [[Template:head#Part of speech]]) '''do not work''' here — and worse, actually have different meanings. For example, {{cd|n}} means {{cd|neuter}}, not {{cd|noun}}; {{cd|pre}} and {{cd|prep}} refer to the {{w|prepositional case}} (found in Russian), not to prepositions; etc. It is best to spell out the parts of speech in full.

==Examples==
* {{m+|de|vertrauen}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[trust]], to place confidence in {{+obj|de|dat|:in(acc)}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[trust]], to place confidence in {{+obj|de|dat|:in(acc)}}
----
* {{m+|de|stoßen}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[bump]] (into something); to [[knock]] (against something) {{+obj|de|:an(acc)|:gegen(acc)}} {{+aux|de|sein}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[bump]] (into something); to [[knock]] (against something) {{+obj|de|:an(acc)|:gegen(acc)}} {{+aux|de|sein}}
----
* {{m+|de|geraten}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[get]] (into); to [[fall]] (into); to [[come]] (to); to [[fly]] (into) {{+obj|de|:in/:unter(acc)&lt;state or condition&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[get]] (into); to [[fall]] (into); to [[come]] (to); to [[fly]] (into) {{+obj|de|:in/:unter(acc)&lt;state or condition&gt;}}
----
* {{m+|de|kloppen}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|informal}} to [[thump]], to [[hit]] hard {{+obj|de|&amp;transitive|&amp;intransitive + :auf}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|informal}} to [[thump]], to [[hit]] hard {{+obj|de|&amp;transitive|&amp;intransitive + :auf}}
----
* {{m+|de|achten}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[care]] about, to [[pay attention]] to {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[care]] about, to [[pay attention]] to {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)}}
----
* {{m+|de|richten}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[direct]], to [[point]] (e.g. a weapon) {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)&lt;at&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[direct]], to [[point]] (e.g. a weapon) {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)&lt;at&gt;}}
----
* {{m+|de|richten}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to be [[directed]] (of emotions, words, etc.) {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)&lt;towards&gt;|:gegen(acc)&lt;at&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to be [[directed]] (of emotions, words, etc.) {{+obj|de|:auf(acc)&lt;towards&gt;|:gegen(acc)&lt;at&gt;}}
----
* {{m+|de|sehen}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[look]] at; to [[watch]] {{+obj|de|:auf&lt;q:often&gt;|:nach&lt;q:also&gt;}} {{ngd|the construction with {{m|de||nach}} often implies a turning of the head; other prepositions can be used depending on the context}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[look]] at; to [[watch]] {{+obj|de|:auf&lt;q:often&gt;|:nach&lt;q:also&gt;}} {{ng|the construction with {{m|de||nach}} often implies a turning of the head; other prepositions can be used depending on the context}}
----
* {{m+|de|bedanken}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to be [[thankful]], to express [[thanks]] {{+obj|de|:für&lt;for&gt; + :bei&lt;to&gt;}}
&lt;/pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to be [[thankful]], to express [[thanks]] {{+obj|de|:für&lt;for&gt; + :bei&lt;to&gt;}}
----
* {{m+|de|beginnen}}
&lt;pre&gt;
# {{lb|de|chiefly|literary}} to [[start]] something; to [[begin]] something {{+obj|de|&amp;transitive|:mit}}
